CAT = "s63Communication"; SEG = "COM"
Q = [
("An agent tells a prospect, \"The state approved me, so you can trust my picks.\" This is:",
 "An unlawful representation", ["Allowed since she is registered", "Allowed only in writing", "A required disclosure"],
 "The USA makes it unlawful to represent that registration means the Administrator has passed on the merits or qualifications of a registrant. Registration is not an endorsement."),
("Which of these may an agent lawfully tell a customer?",
 "She is registered in the state", ["The state approved her skill", "The Administrator vouches for the fund", "Registration makes the stock safe"],
 "Stating the fact of registration is allowed. Saying or implying that registration means approval of the agent's ability or of a security's merits is a prohibited representation."),
("A brochure states that a fund is \"registered with the state, so it is a sound investment.\" This is:",
 "Misleading and prohibited", ["Accurate under the USA", "Allowed with a disclaimer", "Required wording"],
 "Neither the filing of a registration statement nor its effectiveness means the Administrator found it true or passed on the merits of the security, and representing otherwise is unlawful."),
("An agent promises a customer she will make up any loss if a stock falls. This is:",
 "A prohibited guarantee", ["Allowed if put in writing", "Allowed for small trades", "A form of insurance"],
 "Guaranteeing a customer against loss is a prohibited practice under NASAA's ethics policy and FINRA rules. Only a third-party guarantee built into a security, such as a guaranteed bond, may be described."),
("Which statement may an agent lawfully make about a bond?",
 "A parent guarantees its interest", ["It cannot lose value", "Its price will rise by next year", "The state itself stands behind it"],
 "Describing a real third-party guarantee of payment is accurate disclosure. Promising that a security cannot lose value or that its price will rise misrepresents risk."),
("An agent tells a customer a fund has no sales charge when it carries a front-end load. This is:",
 "A material misstatement", ["A minor sales tactic", "Allowed if a prospectus follows", "Puffery, not fraud"],
 "Sales charges are material to a fund purchase. Misstating them violates the antifraud provisions, and later delivery of a prospectus does not cure the misstatement."),
("Under FINRA Rule 4512, a new account record must include:",
 "Whether the customer is of legal age", ["The customer's personal credit score", "The customer's political party", "The customer's tax returns"],
 "Rule 4512 requires the customer's name and residence, whether of legal age, the associated person responsible, the principal's acceptance and, for individual accounts, a reasonable effort to get a trusted contact."),
("A firm must make a reasonable effort to obtain a trusted contact person for:",
 "Non-institutional accounts", ["Only clients over age 65", "Only margin accounts", "Institutional accounts only"],
 "Rule 4512 requires a reasonable effort to obtain a trusted contact for each non-institutional customer account, of any age, when it is opened or updated."),
("SEC Rule 17a-3 requires a firm to send each customer the account record for review at least every:",
 "36 months", ["12 months", "24 months", "60 months"],
 "Rule 17a-3 requires the firm to furnish the account record to the customer within 30 days of opening and at least every 36 months after that, and to send updates when information changes."),
("Under SEC Rule 17a-4, a broker-dealer must keep its blotters and general ledgers for:",
 "Six years", ["Three years", "Two years", "Ten years"],
 "Blotters, general ledgers and customer account ledgers are kept six years. Most other records, including order tickets and business communications, are kept three years."),
("Business emails an agent sends to customers must be kept for at least:",
 "Three years", ["One year", "Six years", "Seven years"],
 "Rule 17a-4 requires communications relating to the business as such to be kept three years, the first two in an easily accessible place."),
("An agent texts a customer about a trade from a personal messaging app the firm cannot capture. This mainly breaks rules on:",
 "Keeping business records", ["Customer margin requirements", "Net capital", "Options position limits"],
 "Business communications must be preserved under Rule 17a-4 whatever the channel. Using off-channel apps that the firm cannot capture and supervise breaks recordkeeping and supervision rules."),
("Under Regulation S-P, a customer may opt out of the firm sharing nonpublic personal information with:",
 "Nonaffiliated third parties", ["Regulators examining the firm", "The firm's own employees", "Courts under a subpoena"],
 "Regulation S-P requires notice and an opt-out before sharing nonpublic personal information with nonaffiliated third parties, subject to exceptions such as regulatory examinations and legal process."),
("Under the 2024 amendments to Regulation S-P, customers whose sensitive information was likely accessed without authorization must be notified within:",
 "30 days", ["7 days", "60 days", "90 days"],
 "The amended rule requires notice to affected individuals as soon as practicable, but no later than 30 days after the firm becomes aware that unauthorized access occurred or is reasonably likely to have occurred."),
("Amended Regulation S-P requires a broker-dealer to have:",
 "A written incident response program", ["Cyber insurance for every client", "An SEC-approved firewall", "A privacy officer in every branch"],
 "The 2024 amendments require written policies and procedures for an incident response program to detect, respond to and recover from unauthorized access to customer information."),
("Under the USA, a registered broker-dealer's records may be examined by the Administrator:",
 "At any time, if reasonable", ["Only once a year", "Only with a court order in hand", "Only after a complaint"],
 "The USA makes records subject at any time or from time to time to reasonable periodic, special or other examinations by the Administrator, within or outside the state."),
("Under Regulation T, a customer buying $46,000 of marginable stock must deposit at least:",
 "$23,000", ["$11,500", "$2,000", "$46,000"],
 "Regulation T's initial requirement is 50% of the purchase: $46,000 x 50% = $23,000. $11,500 would be 25%, the maintenance level for long positions, not the initial deposit."),
("A new margin customer buys $1,500 of stock. Under Regulation T and FINRA's minimum equity rule, she must deposit:",
 "$1,500", ["$750", "$2,000", "$375"],
 "FINRA Rule 4210 requires $2,000 of equity in a new margin account, but never more than the purchase price. For a purchase under $2,000 the customer pays in full, here $1,500. $750 would be Reg T's 50% alone."),
("A cash account customer buys stock and sells it before paying for the purchase, then never pays. Under Regulation T the account is generally:",
 "Frozen for 90 days", ["Frozen for 30 days", "Closed for good", "Converted to margin"],
 "This is free riding. Regulation T freezes the cash account for 90 days, during which the customer must have the full purchase price in the account before any buy is executed."),
("Which margin document lets the firm pledge customer securities as collateral for its own loan?",
 "Hypothecation agreement", ["Loan consent", "Margin credit agreement", "Options account agreement"],
 "The hypothecation agreement permits the firm to pledge (rehypothecate) margin securities to a bank. The loan consent lets the firm lend them to others, and the credit agreement discloses interest terms."),
("Which margin account document may a customer decline to sign and still trade on margin?",
 "The loan consent", ["The credit agreement", "The hypothecation agreement", "The signed margin agreement"],
 "The loan consent, allowing the firm to lend the customer's securities to short sellers, is optional. The credit and hypothecation agreements are required for a margin account."),
("Under FINRA Rule 2360, a firm must approve a customer's account for options trading before:",
 "The first options order", ["Any stock trade", "The 15th day of trading", "The first exercise notice"],
 "A principal must approve the account for options before any options transaction. The signed options agreement may follow within 15 days of approval."),
("A call with a $50 strike trades at $7 when the stock is $54. Its time value is:",
 "$3", ["$4", "$7", "$11"],
 "Intrinsic value is $54 - $50 = $4. Time value is the premium minus intrinsic value: $7 - $4 = $3."),
("A put with a $40 strike is on a stock trading at $36. Its intrinsic value is:",
 "$4", ["$0", "$36", "$40"],
 "A put is in the money when the stock is below the strike. Intrinsic value is $40 - $36 = $4."),
("A call with a $25 strike trades at $1.50 when the stock is $23. Its intrinsic value is:",
 "$0", ["$1.50", "$2", "$3.50"],
 "A call has intrinsic value only when the stock is above the strike. At $23 this $25 call is out of the money, so its intrinsic value is $0 and the whole $1.50 premium is time value."),
("A customer buys a put with a $65 strike for a $4 premium. Her breakeven stock price is:",
 "$61", ["$69", "$65", "$4"],
 "A put buyer breaks even at the strike minus the premium: $65 - $4 = $61. Adding the premium ($69) is the breakeven for a call."),
("Under FINRA Rule 2210, a written message sent to 20 retail investors within 30 days is:",
 "Correspondence", ["A retail communication", "An institutional communication", "Research needing filing"],
 "Correspondence is a written or electronic communication sent to 25 or fewer retail investors within a 30-day period. More than 25 makes it a retail communication."),
("An agent's LinkedIn profile summary describing her services is:",
 "Static content needing approval", ["Interactive content like a chat", "Exempt from supervision", "Private correspondence"],
 "Static social media content such as a profile, banner or background is treated as a retail communication and must be approved by a principal before posting."),
("A firm edits a customer's favorable post about it and then reposts it. The firm has:",
 "Become entangled with it", ["No responsibility for it", "Created correspondence", "Filed it with FINRA"],
 "A firm that involves itself in preparing or editing third-party content is entangled with it and is responsible for it as its own communication."),
("An agent posts about a fund on a social media account she also uses for business. The firm must:",
 "Retain the post as a record", ["Ignore it as a personal post", "Delete it after a day", "Report it to the SEC"],
 "Business communications on social media are subject to the same recordkeeping and supervision rules as other communications and must be retained."),
("A broker-dealer's public website describing its services is:",
 "A retail communication", ["Correspondence", "An institutional communication", "A private record"],
 "Content available to any retail investor, such as a public website, is a retail communication under Rule 2210 and needs principal approval before posting."),
("A retail communication compares a bond fund with bank CDs but omits that the fund is not insured. Under FINRA Rule 2210 it is:",
 "Misleading by omission", ["Fair and balanced", "Allowed in a bond ad", "Required wording"],
 "Rule 2210 requires communications to be fair and balanced. Comparing a fund with insured deposits without disclosing the difference in risk and insurance omits a material fact."),
("Under FINRA Rule 2210, retail communications generally may NOT:",
 "Project a specific future return", ["Show past results in context", "Name the member firm", "Include a phone number"],
 "Predictions or projections of investment performance are generally prohibited, apart from limited exceptions such as hypothetical illustrations of mathematical principles. Fair past performance with context is allowed."),
("Every retail communication must prominently show:",
 "The member firm's name", ["The agent's home address", "Each customer's account number", "The Administrator's seal"],
 "Rule 2210 requires retail communications to prominently disclose the name of the member firm, or a fictional name it uses that is filed with FINRA."),
("A wrap fee program charges customers:",
 "One fee for advice and trades", ["A commission per trade only", "A fee only when it profits", "Nothing for advice or trades"],
 "A wrap fee program bundles advisory services and trade execution for a single fee, usually a percentage of assets. Its sponsor must describe the program and its costs to clients."),
("A wrap fee account is least likely to suit a customer who:",
 "Trades rarely and buys and holds", ["Trades often and actively", "Wants ongoing professional advice", "Prefers a single bill"],
 "Because the wrap fee is charged regardless of activity, a buy-and-hold investor who trades rarely may pay more than with commissions, a point regulators expect firms to consider."),
("When a broker-dealer sells securities at a bank branch, customers must be told the products are:",
 "Not FDIC insured", ["Backed by the bank", "Federally guaranteed", "Covered by deposit insurance"],
 "NASAA's rules for sales at financial institutions require disclosure that the products are not insured by the FDIC, are not deposits or obligations of the bank, are not bank guaranteed and may lose value."),
("Under NASAA's rules for securities sales at financial institutions, sales should take place:",
 "Away from the teller area", ["At the teller windows", "In the open lobby only", "Only by phone, never in person"],
 "The rules call for securities activities to be conducted in a location physically distinct from where retail deposits are taken, to reduce confusion between deposits and investments."),
("An agent recommending a new issue of a registered mutual fund must deliver:",
 "A prospectus", ["The fund's tax returns", "A copy of her Form U4", "The Administrator's approval"],
 "Under the Securities Act of 1933, a prospectus must be delivered for a registered offering such as fund shares. No Administrator approval exists to deliver, since registration is not approval."),
("A customer buys $40,000 of stock on margin with a $20,000 debit. Under the 25% maintenance rule, a call comes when the value falls below about:",
 "$26,667", ["$30,000", "$20,000", "$25,000"],
 "The maintenance point for a long account is the debit divided by 0.75: $20,000 / 0.75 = $26,667. At that value equity is $6,667, which is 25% of market value."),
]
